 **GPU test** checks a graphics-card undervolt, overclock or VRAM offset: it runs work whose correct result is known on the chosen GPU (never an unnamed "first" one, so laptops pick the discrete card) for 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 minutes (default 10) and counts every wrong result, as you, no password. Run **VRAM** after changing a memory offset (it fills 10–95 %, default 80 %, of the video memory) and **Core** after changing a core curve. The screen may stutter meanwhile. Needs the Vulkan driver of your GPU. A lost GPU, any wrong result, or an Xid / amdgpu error reported by the Monitor during the run is a failure. A pass is evidence, not proof. Each result is kept (newest 50, `~/.local/state/centurion/gpustress.json`) with the curve state in effect. If the kernel log needs a password on this machine, Xid / amdgpu errors are not counted during the test, the section says so and each result is marked; only the tool's own result counts.
 
+**CPU test** checks a Curve Optimizer offset (AMD) or an undervolt (Intel), as you, no password. **Per core** moves one thread from core to core (1, 3, 6 or 10 minutes each, default 3; *Rounds* repeats the whole tour): a lone thread runs at the highest boost, where a too-negative offset of one core fails first. Use it after per-core offsets. **All cores** loads every CPU for 5–60 minutes: use it after an all-core offset. The load is **SSE** (highest clock, the best first test), **AVX2** or **AVX-512** (more heat and current, lower clock), only those the CPU has. *Idle ↔ load transitions* (on by default) spends the last third of each core's time in short bursts with pauses, because offsets often fail while a core leaves an idle state and boosts. Every result is compared with a reference computed on two other cores; a wrong result, or a machine check or lockup reported by the Monitor during the run, is a failure, and the failing CPUs are named — on AMD with their CCD and the Ryzen Curve slot (*CPU 5 (CCD0 slot 5)*); make those slots less negative and test again. An unstable offset may freeze or reset the machine instead: the test is stored as running while it runs, so after the reboot the section shows *machine went down* with the core that was loaded, and a notification says so. On AMD, *Hold the "CO validation" preset during the test* (on by default; asks for the password) applies that Optimizations preset for the test and returns the tuning from before afterwards (not while a game holds the tuning). Each result is kept (newest 50, `~/.local/state/centurion/cpustress.json`) with the CPU curve in effect. A Ryzen Curve profile that was applied by name (loaded or saved and applied unchanged, from the tray or a scene) and then tested shows **✓ verified** — or **✗ failed** / **✗ crashed** — next to it in the Ryzen Curve tab; saving the profile again clears the badge, since the tested offsets changed.
