 **GPU test** (`crates/centurion-gpustress`, `gui/src/gpustress.cpp` logic + `tests/gpustress_test.cpp`, `gui/src/gpustressbox.cpp`; spec `docs/GPUSTRESS.md`): Health → Curve stability. Vendor-neutral Vulkan 1.1 compute, run as the user; the tool never touches clocks or curves, it loads the GPU while the user's curve is in effect. `ash` loads `libvulkan.so.1` at run time (none → `init_failed`); the WGSL shaders in `shaders/` are compiled to SPIR-V by `naga` in `build.rs`. `centurion-gpustress list` prints the devices (PCI address via `VK_EXT_pci_bus_info`, else a unique vendor/device match in sysfs; CPU implementations as `"type":"cpu"`, hidden by the GUI); `vram --device <pci> --percent 10..95 --seconds <s>` and `core --device <pci> --seconds <s>` print JSON lines `start` / `progress` / `error` / `end` (16 error lines per pass, 200 per run; exit 0 pass, 1 errors, 2 device lost, 3 init failure, 4 stopped by SIGTERM/SIGINT). VRAM: chunks of at most 256 MiB from the `DEVICE_LOCAL` heap (share of the `VK_EXT_memory_budget` budget, keeping 256 MiB free), one fenced submission per chunk and dispatch (a fence not signalled within 10 s is a hung GPU: `device_lost`, exit 2); patterns addr / walk1 / walk0 / solid / random are a stateless function of (pattern, global word index, pass), so the check recomputes the expected word and keeps no copy. Core: 1 Mi invocations of dependent f32 FMA + integer mixing, the first two runs must agree and become the reference, every later run is compared with it on the GPU; one dispatch is calibrated to about 100 ms and the seed (new reference pair, recalibration) changes every 60 s. Build feature `fault-inject` (tests only) corrupts one word after the write of pass 3 / disturbs one invocation. The GUI follows the Memory test: verdict pass / fail / incomplete, `xid` (not 45 / 154) and `amdgpu` events from `CurvePage::onFault` count as faults, the run is killed `seconds + 120` s after start, the verdict is decided 3 s after the tool exits so the kernel events the failure caused (delayed by the Monitor's debounce) still count, and it is not offered while an NVIDIA dGPU is on the bus but unusable. With a restricted kernel log (`CurvePage::setKernelLog(false)`, see Memory test) the box shows a note that GPU errors are not counted and each result gets `"kernel_log": false`. Results: `$XDG_STATE_HOME/centurion/gpustress.json`, JSON array, newest 50, with the GPU curve state in effect at start.
 
+**CPU test** (`crates/centurion-cpustress`, `gui/src/cpustress.cpp` logic + `tests/cpustress_test.cpp`, `gui/src/cpustressbox.cpp`): Health → Curve stability. Own crate because the AVX-512 intrinsics need Rust 1.89. Run as the user; touches no clocks or curves. `info` prints the online physical cores (`thread_siblings_list`, first logical CPU first, L3 id) and the loads this CPU runs (`sse` always; `avx2` = AVX2 + FMA; `avx512` = AVX-512F, runtime detection). `percore --load L --seconds S [--cycles N] [--transitions] [--cpus LIST]` pins one thread (`sched_setaffinity`, checked with `sched_getcpu`) to the first logical CPU of each core in turn; `allcore --load L --seconds S [--transitions]` one thread per online logical CPU. Work: a chunk (about 15 ms) fills a 256 KiB buffer from its seed (8 seeds, rotating), then iterates the logistic map x ← 3.99·(x − x²) on 8 / 16 / 32 doubles (scalar SSE2 / 256-bit FMA / 512-bit FMA), an integer multiply-shift chain that absorbs every double's bits before each step (so a wrong bit cannot be rounded away), and a dependent load-xor-store walk through the buffer; the result is a 64-bit hash. Before the test the 8 reference hashes are computed on two CPUs at once (the first and last tested); on disagreement a third decides per seed and the outvoted CPU counts as failed; no majority = `fail`. A CPU's first wrong chunk ends its turn (per-core: the next core; all-core: that thread stops). `--transitions`: the last third of each core's time (all-core: of the run) sleeps 1–200 ms after every chunk. JSON lines `start` / `core` / `progress` (1 Hz) / `error` (200 lines max) / `core_end` / `end` with `failed` CPUs; exit 0 pass, 1 errors, 3 init failure or bad arguments, 4 stopped (SIGTERM/SIGINT, checked after every chunk). Build feature `fault-inject` (tests only) corrupts the 4th chunk on the highest-numbered CPU. GUI: verdict as the GPU test; `mce` and `lockup` events from `CurvePage::onFault` count as faults and the CPU they name (`CPU 3:` / `CPU#3`) is listed; the run is killed `total + 120` s after start; verdict 3 s after exit. The record is stored as `"verdict": "running"` (with `boot` and the current `cpu`) at start and at every `core` line; on the next start of the GUI, `settle()` turns such records into `crashed` (another boot: the machine went down with that CPU loaded; a notification is raised) or `incomplete` (same boot). AMD only: *Hold the "CO validation" preset* sends tune-helper `{"op": "apply", "mode": "game", "preset": "CO validation", "values": OptimizeTab::builtinValues(…), "owner_pid": <GUI pid>}` before the run and `{"op": "release", "owner_pid": …}` after it: the game-session layer restores the previous tuning, and a dead GUI's session is pruned like a dead launcher's. Records: `$XDG_STATE_HOME/centurion/cpustress.json`, newest 50, with `curve` = `CurvePage::currentState("cpu")` (Curve Optimizer or Intel undervolt journal state), `vendor`, and for a `profile:NAME` curve on AMD `profile_hash` (first 16 hex digits of the SHA-1 of the profile file). The Ryzen Curve tab shows the newest pass / fail / crashed record whose curve and hash match the selected profile (`cpustress::verification`). For that, the Ryzen tab now names the profile in `set_coall` / `set_coper_batch` (`"profile"`, journalled as `profile:NAME` by ryzen-co-helper) while the grid is unchanged since the profile was loaded or saved.
+
+**PL1 tau** (`legion_wmi::status`, Firmware tab): WMAE get `0x01070000` returns the EC's F5E4 decoded to seconds (20, 24, 28, 32, 40, 48, 56, 64, 80, 96, 112, 128, 160; default 56). The SMCN19WW DSDT stores a set only in the EC and passes it nowhere else, and the encoding is Intel's time-window format, so on AMD the effect of a write is unknown; the value is reported as `pl1_tau_s` (when > 0) and shown read-only.
+
+**NTSYNC** (`centurion-gamemode`, Optimizations → System at game start): tune.json `"ntsync": true` makes WRAP / RUN set `PROTON_USE_NTSYNC=1` before exec when `/dev/ntsync` is readable and writable for the user (`access(R_OK|W_OK)`) and the variable is not set already; otherwise a log line. The GUI shows whether the device exists and is usable.
+
 **Curve journal and stability** (`ocjournal.rs` in both crates, `gui/src/curvepage.cpp`): ryzen-co-helper, intel-uv-helper, nvcurve-root-helper and `nvcurve autoload` append `{"t","boot","kind","state"}` to `/var/lib/centurion/oc-journal.jsonl` (0644, rotated at 256 KiB) after every successful curve write. Health → Curve stability builds per-boot timelines (boot start / end from `exposure.json`, stock from each boot's start) and attributes the saved Xid / GSP / D3cold (GPU) and MCE / lockup (CPU) lines of `health.log` to the state in effect; Xid 45 / 154 are follow-ups and not counted. Guard: a new Xid 13/31/43/61/62/69/109/119/120 with a non-stock GPU curve raises a notification and — opt-in — runs `reset_gpu_curve` (never after Xid 79, never while the dGPU is unusable); an MCE / lockup with a non-stock CPU curve does the same with the CPU helper's `reset`. At most one reset per minute.
 
